From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 02:44:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 04/15] Encrypted volumes from Haiku: EncryptedVolume, Tracker,
 DriveSetup

EncryptedVolume (src/apps/encryptedvolume, in system/apps):
- "EncryptedVolume <partition>" asks for the passphrase (read-only
  option), unlocks it in a worker thread, mounts what's inside and opens
  it in Tracker; a wrong passphrase is said in the window;
- "--encrypt <partition>" asks for a name and the passphrase twice,
  formats LUKS2 (Argon2id measured for 2 s), puts BFS on it through the
  disk device manager and mounts it;
- "--lock <partition>" unmounts and locks.
Its windows answer 'unlk' and 'encr' scripting messages for tests.

Tracker's Mount menu lists locked LUKS2 partitions as "Unlock ...", which
starts EncryptedVolume. DriveSetup's Partition menu has Unlock..., Lock
(not for the boot volume) and Encrypt... . Both recognise LUKS2 by its
signature (luks2_partitions.cpp, no crypto), as DriveSetup did for its
lock icon: a disk-system add-on would make mount_server and DriveSetup
try to mount the container.

luks_disk: the decrypted device is removable and B_EJECT_DEVICE locks
it, so mount_server mounts the volume when it's unlocked and locks it
after Tracker unmounted it, with no change to either. The volume is
opened without the driver's lock held (open() and close() of a device
take the device manager's lock, which init/uninit_driver hold when they
take ours: a deadlock a review found).

luks2_userland.cpp: random numbers and the KDF choice, shared by the luks
command and EncryptedVolume.

Installer needed no change: an unlocked volume is a valid target and its
"Install EFI loader" puts the loader on the ESP.

Tested in QEMU, src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/run-ui-test.sh (driven over
ssh with hey): encrypt, unmount (locks), unlock through Tracker with a
wrong and the right passphrase, lock, encrypt a GPT partition, install
onto it with Installer, install the EFI loader, then boot that disk on
its own with one passphrase prompt: 14 + 3 + 5 checks.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
